  [
    {
      "question": "What happens if I upload the same image twice?",
      "answer": "Re-uploading byte-identical content to the same newsletter returns the existing image record instead of creating a new one — the response is still a `201` with the original `id` and `image` URL. Uploads with different bytes, or the same bytes in a different newsletter, create new records as usual. The `metadata` you pass on the duplicate upload is ignored; the original record's metadata is preserved."
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the metadata field for?",
      "answer": "The `metadata` field lets you store arbitrary key-value data on an image — for example, the original filename or alt text. Pass it as a JSON string in the `metadata` form field when uploading. It's returned in list and detail responses, so you can use it to track provenance."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does Buttondown clean up unused images automatically?",
      "answer": "No. Images persist until you explicitly delete them via the API. If you want to find unreferenced images, you can compare the list of uploaded images against image URLs in your emails."
    }
  ]
---

## Supported formats

Buttondown accepts PNG, JPG, GIF, and WebP images. Other formats are rejected by default because email clients and social preview crawlers do not render them reliably.

If you upload an unsupported image, the API will return a `422` with:

```json
{
  "detail": [
    {
      "type": "value_error.file_extension",
      "loc": ["file", "image"],
      "msg": "Unsupported image type. Please upload a PNG, JPG, GIF, or WebP image."
    }
  ]
}
```

### Bypassing format validation

If you know what you're doing (e.g. your file is only used on the web archive, not in emails or link previews), you can bypass this restriction by providing a `X-Buttondown-Live-Dangerously` header with a value of `true`:

```bash
curl -X POST "https://api.buttondown.com/v1/images" \
  -H "Authorization: Token YOUR_API_KEY" \
  -H "X-Buttondown-Live-Dangerously: true" \
  -F "image=@logo.svg"
```

